course: everyday
lesson: 3
title: Lifting and carrying heavy things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your back is strong, but bending over with straight legs to lift something heavy puts a big strain on it; your leg muscles are much stronger and are made for the job.
explain: To lift well: stand close to the object with your feet apart, bend your knees, keep your back straight, hold the object close to your body and stand up using your legs.
explain: Turn by moving your feet, not by twisting your back, and put the load down the same way, bending your knees.
explain: The farther a heavy thing is from your body, the harder it pulls on your back, just as a full bucket is easier to hold against your tummy than at arm's length.
explain: If something is too heavy, ask an adult or a friend to help, or use a trolley; a girl in Phoenix moving a big bag of soil asks her dad to take one end instead of dragging it alone.
explain: A boy in Manchester tried to lift a full box of books by bending his back with straight legs; something pulled in his lower back and he could not play football for weeks.
explain: A girl in Sydney carried a huge backpack on one shoulder every day for months; her shoulder and back began to ache, and the school nurse helped her pack it lighter and wear both straps.
fact: Your leg muscles are some of the strongest muscles in your body.
fact: To lift something heavy, bend your knees and keep your back straight.
fact: Holding a load close to your body makes it easier to carry and safer for your back.
fact: Twisting your back while holding something heavy can hurt it; turn with your feet instead.
fact: If something is too heavy for you, ask for help instead of lifting it alone.
fact: A trolley, a cart or wheels can move heavy things without lifting them.
fact: A school backpack should be worn on both shoulders.
fact: Heavy books are best packed close to your back in a backpack.
fact: A backpack that is too heavy makes you lean forward and can make your back and shoulders hurt.
fact: Two light bags, one in each hand, are easier to carry than one heavy bag in one hand.
fact: Back injuries from lifting wrongly can take weeks or months to heal.
fact: If your back starts to hurt while you lift or carry, stop at once and tell an adult; pain is a warning, not a sign of getting stronger.
fact: A school backpack should weigh no more than about a tenth to a seventh of your body weight.
exercise: Which muscles should do most of the work when you lift something heavy? | Your legs | Your back and tummy | Your neck and shoulders | Your fingers
exercise: How should you bend down to pick up a heavy box? | Bend your knees and keep your back straight | Keep your legs straight and bend your back | Bend sideways at the waist | Twist your back and reach down
exercise: Where should you hold a heavy load? | Close to your body | At arm's length in front of you | High above your head | Out to one side of you
exercise: Why is a full bucket easier to carry close to your body? | It pulls less on your back | It becomes lighter | The water stops moving and sloshing about | Your arms grow stronger
exercise: You are holding a heavy box and need to turn. What should you do? | Turn by moving your feet | Twist your back quickly | Swing the box around | Throw the box where you want it
exercise: A box is too heavy for you to lift. What should you do? | Ask someone to help you | Pull hard with your back | Lift it quickly with a jerk | Kick it along the floor instead
exercise: How should you wear a school backpack? | On both shoulders | Over just one shoulder | Hanging from your elbow | Very low on your bottom
exercise: Where in the backpack should the heaviest books go? | Close to your back | In the outside pocket | At the very top, far from you | Tied on the outside
exercise: What can happen if your backpack is far too heavy? | Your back and shoulders can hurt | You get taller faster because you stretch | Your legs grow weaker | Nothing at all happens
exercise: What is easier to carry? | Two light bags, one in each hand | One heavy bag carried in one hand | One heavy bag hooked on one finger | Two heavy bags held together in one hand
exercise: What helps you move a heavy box without lifting it? | A trolley with wheels | A thin plastic bag | A piece of string | A pair of thick gardening gloves
exercise: How should you put a heavy box down? | Bend your knees and lower it close to you | Drop it from waist height and step back fast | Bend your back and let go | Throw it gently forward
exercise: Where should your feet be before you lift? | Close to the object and a little apart | Far away from the object, so you can reach | Pressed tightly together | On tiptoe
exercise: Why should you not lift something heavy with a quick jerk? | A sudden pull can strain your muscles | Jerking makes the object heavier | It makes the floor slippery | It wakes up the neighbours
test: A boy in Glasgow sees his gran bending with straight legs to lift a bag of potatoes. What could he tell her? | Bend your knees and keep it close | Lift it faster so it is over sooner | Hold it out in front with straight arms | Twist round to lift it sideways
test: Two children carry the same heavy bag. One holds it against his chest, the other with straight arms in front. Who is safer? | The one holding it against his chest | The one with straight arms | They are exactly the same | Neither, bags are never heavy enough to hurt
test: Which is true? | Your legs are stronger than your back for lifting | Your back is the best muscle for lifting | Bending your knees makes lifting harder | Close loads are heavier than far ones
test: Ella has to move a crate of water bottles across the garage. What is the wisest way? | Put it on a trolley and wheel it | Carry it on one shoulder | Lift it over her head | Drag it along the floor with a twisted back
test: Your friend carries his heavy backpack on one shoulder every day. What might happen? | His shoulder and back may start to hurt | He will grow taller on that side than the other | His bag will get lighter | Nothing ever happens
test: You hold a heavy pot of soil and need to put it on a shelf to your left. What do you do? | Step round with your feet to face it | Twist your waist to the left | Throw it onto the shelf | Swing it round with straight arms
test: Why do removal workers bend their knees when they pick up boxes? | Their strong leg muscles do the work | It makes the boxes lighter | It is a rule only for adults | It helps them see the box better from close up
test: What would be the wisest thing if a sofa needs moving? | Several people lift it together | One child pulls it alone | Push it over and roll it | Lift one end by bending your back
test: Leo has a heavy shopping bag. How can he make carrying easier? | Split it into two bags, one for each hand | Hold it on one finger | Hold it far out to the side | Carry it on his head without holding it
test: dad's like lift that box, with ur legs not ur back. how | Bend your knees, keep your back straight | Bend over with straight legs | Twist round as you lift | Hold it far out in front of you with both arms
test: holding the heavy box, need to turn round, just twist? | No, turn with your feet instead | Yes, twisting is quickest | Yes, if you twist fast | Yes, but lean right back first so it is light
test: backpack on 1 shoulder looks cooler tbh | Wear it on both shoulders for your back | One shoulder is better for your back | It does not matter at all | Swap shoulders every few years to keep it even
test: nan's shopping, 1 massive bag or 2 smaller ones | Two bags, one in each hand | One big heavy bag in one hand | One bag balanced on your head | Drag the big bag on the ground
test: dad wants me to carry the telly to the car by myself | Ask for help, it is too heavy to lift alone | Lift it fast with a straight back | Drag it across the floor on your own | Carry it on your head to balance it
test: my back hurts after moving the boxes, its not bad tho | Stop lifting and tell an adult about the pain | Keep going so you finish sooner | Lift the heavier ones to stretch it | Ignore it, pain always means you are getting stronger
test: backpack's like half my weight lol is that ok | No, it is far too heavy and can hurt your back | Yes, it makes your muscles stronger | Yes, if you only use one strap | Yes, if you walk fast to school and back every day
test: my uncle's mate lifted a fridge on his own and his back went pop and he's never walked again apparently | The real lesson is to get help with heavy things; «never walked again» is likely exaggerated | The real lesson is that fridges should never be moved at all | It proves lifting only hurts adults, never children | It must be exactly true because it is so specific
test: they reckon at school if your backpack's too heavy your spine goes permanently bendy forever | That is likely blown up; a heavy bag can make your back ache, so pack it light and wear both straps | It is fully true, one heavy day bends the spine for life | It is true, so backpacks should be carried on one shoulder | It is true, so children should never carry anything
test: Noah couldn't lift the box in PE and the big kids called him weak, gonna tell everyone lol | No, asking for help with a heavy load is sensible, and mocking him is unkind | Yes, because he should have lifted it alone | Yes, since being strong matters most at school | Yes, but only to the kids in his team
exam: Identify the posture which ought to be adopted for the purpose of raising a heavy object from the floor. | The knees bent and the back maintained upright and straight | The knees kept straight and the back bent forward from the hips | The body twisted to one side whilst the arms are extended | The object raised at arm's length away from the body
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the proper manner of wearing a school backpack? | It should be worn upon both shoulders, the heavier books being placed nearest the back | It should be worn upon one shoulder in order to rest the other | It should be worn loosely so that it hangs below the waist | It should be held in one hand by its top loop
exam: It being given that an object is too heavy for a child to raise unaided, select the course of action consistent with safe practice. | The child should request assistance or employ a trolley or other wheeled device | The child should attempt to raise it with a sudden jerk | The child should raise it whilst turning the back sharply | The child should lift it repeatedly until it moves
exam: Whereas a load must be turned through ninety degrees whilst being held, determine the method that best protects the back. | The turning of the whole body by means of the feet | The twisting of the back whilst the feet remain still | The swinging of the load at arm's length | The bending sideways from the waist
